    description: "**Mechanic**: The catalog output line-format is `<name> (core|extra): <description>`. Core entries are intended as primary, bundled, first-class analyzers; extras are optional add-ons. The implementation populates descriptions for 63 of 63 extras but only ~63 of 115 core entries, leaving 52 core rows as bare `<name> (core):`. The help text advertises the command as showing \"which languages and frameworks hypergumbo can analyze.\"\n\n**Expectation**: Every catalog entry carries a description distinguishing \"bundled tree-sitter via grammar X\" from \"stub registration with no analyzer logic\" so the consumer can plan language coverage.\n\n**Scope**: 52 of 115 (45%) core catalog entries with empty descriptions, including `apex`, `asm`, `astro`, `bibtex`, `bitbake`, `circom`, `commonlisp`, `fennel`, `gitignore`, `gleam`, `hack`, `pony`, and others.\n\n**Exposure**: Any developer or agent reading catalog output to decide whether a given tech stack will be analyzed.\n\n**Consequence**: The reader cannot tell core-but-stub apart from core-and-fully-implemented; language-coverage planning depends on inspecting source rather than reading documented output — a Tier 3 decision-corrupting gap.\n\n**Source**: F43.A3 (INDEPENDENT, HIGH).\n\nFiled via tranch 03 dogfood (passes 41-60, substrate sha256 a32c4a31..., HEAD 9a18959071). Tranch state: `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/dogfood_tranch_03/tranch_state.json`."  # b2b9
    fields: {}  # b2b9
